WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:06.800
Flying submarines are a common idea both in
science fiction and in real life.

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:11.440
But this robotic insect may be the smallest one out there.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.100
Researchers have built a small robot,

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:18.300
that can move as well underwater as it can in the air.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:23.199
The small machine was adapted from an earlier
design that could only fly,

00:00:23.199 --> 00:00:27.240
but in order to make it swim, they had to go back to the drawing board.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:32.940
First, it needed help staying oriented while underwater.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:38.960
The bot only weighs 175 milligrams, about
the same as a raindrop,

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.180
so to keep it from losing control, they added underwater stabilizers,

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.180
and slowed down the wingbeats.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:51.420
When flying, the robot’s wings flap about
250 times a second,

00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:55.979
To keep the robot from spinning around wildly
in the water, they slow it down to around

00:00:55.979 --> 00:00:58.789
9 beats per second while submerged.

00:00:58.789 --> 00:01:02.620
But the robot needs a way to get out of the
water once it’s underneath.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.990
To solve this problem, the researchers turned
to electrolysis.

00:01:07.990 --> 00:01:13.240
Electrolysis is a chemical reaction jump started
by an electric current.

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:20.200
In water, electrolysis breaks up the H2O molecules
into hydrogen and oxygen.

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:27.579
The researchers attached an electrolysis device
to the robot, as well as an electric “sparker”

00:01:27.579 --> 00:01:34.759
When the apparatus is activated, the robot
creates a buildup of gas in a chamber beneath itself.

00:01:34.759 --> 00:01:41.490
And when the sparker ignites, the reaction rockets
the bot to a height of 30 centimeters.

00:01:41.490 --> 00:01:47.300
Even though it can’t fly when wet, the robot
can still glide safely back to land,

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.240
and when it’s dry, take to the skies once again.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.120
While you won’t see this bot flying or swimming
in your neighborhood soon,

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:58.620
the researchers are confident that in the future,

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:07.500
this robotic insect could be used for help in disaster cleanup or environmental surveys.

